// The type check of the acceptance suite: the specs in "Tests/Acceptance/" and
// the Playwright configuration next to this file, which are one island across
// two directories.
//
// It inherits every strictness option from "../tsconfig.json" and changes two
// things.
//
// "types": ["node"], because a spec imports "node:fs", "node:path" and
// "node:sqlite" — the same reason "Build/Tests/TypeScript/tsconfig.json" gives,
// and the same reason this is a third project rather than a wider "include" in
// the first one. The sources say "types": [] and mean it: the modules run in a
// browser, and pulling the node ambient types into their program would let a
// source file use "process" and still type check.
//
// "paths", because TypeScript resolves a bare import by walking "node_modules"
// upwards from the **importing file**, and a spec in "Tests/Acceptance/" walks
// past nothing that has one — the runner is installed here, from the manifest
// next to this file, and deliberately not in a "node_modules" at the repository
// root. That is the same gap "runTests.sh" closes for the run itself with
// NODE_PATH; a compiler option is what closes it for the type check. Paths
// without "baseUrl" are resolved relative to this file (TypeScript 4.4+).
//
// The consequence is that "Build/playwright/node_modules" has to exist before
// this project can be checked, which is why the "typecheckJs" suite installs
// that manifest as well.
//
// Run by "npm run typecheck" in "Build/", which checks all three projects.
{
    "extends": "../tsconfig.json",
    "compilerOptions": {
        "types": [
            "node"
        ],
        "paths": {
            "@playwright/test": [
                "./node_modules/@playwright/test"
            ]
        }
    },
    "include": [
        "./*.ts",
        "../../Tests/Acceptance/**/*.ts"
    ]
}
